# Byte-exactness, scoped to the files this research project owns.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on this machine, so by default Git rewrites LF to
# CRLF on checkout. That is normally harmless, but not here: every run records
# a SHA-256 per source file in `artifacts/runs/*/source_manifest.json`, and
# `expkit/source.py` rebuilds a tree and recomputes those hashes to prove the
# run can be restored. A checkout that silently changed the bytes would make
# every one of those hashes wrong, and the failure would look like tampering
# rather than like a line-ending setting.
#
# The rules are PATH-SCOPED on purpose. This repository is a fork of the
# MMDocRAG benchmark, and a blanket `* -text` would restage ~700 MB of upstream
# dataset and response files whose blobs are already normalised -- rewriting
# history that this project neither produced nor needs to guarantee. Only the
# paths added by this project are pinned.
# This file must pin ITSELF first. Git parses .gitattributes line by line, and
# a trailing carriage return makes the last token on every line read as
# `-text\r`, which is not an attribute -- so a .gitattributes checked out with
# CRLF silently disables every rule it contains, including all of the byte
# pins below. The working copy on the machine this was written on happened to
# be LF, so the failure only appears on a fresh clone, where it looks like the
# recorded SHA-256 of every source file is wrong. Pinning this file keeps the
# rules alive wherever the repository is checked out.
.gitattributes  -text
canonical/**    -text
expkit/**       -text
retrieval/**    -text
router/**       -text
tests/**        -text
docs/**         -text
manifests/**    -text
artifacts/**    -text
experiments.py  -text
manifest.py     -text
cost_estimate.py -text
eval_e29_paired.py -text
requirements.txt -text

# The source bundle is the only artefact that can actually reproduce a run's
# source, so no filter or diff driver may ever touch it.
*.zip binary
*.sqlite binary
*.npz binary
*.pkl binary
